criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
README `Installation` and `docs/02-getting-started.md` "Adding the dependency":

- show `rayx_reactive = { git = "https://github.com/rayx-ui/RayX.Reactive", tag = "v1.0.0" }` (getting started also with `features = ["diagnostics"]` after the tag), say to use the latest release tag from the repository's Releases page, that `Cargo.lock` records the tag's commit, and that the crate is released only as Git tags (not on crates.io) and consumed only by release tag: no branch, `rev` or path dependency;
- drop "not yet published on crates.io", "Once it is published ... 0.1" and getting started's `path = "../RayX.Reactive/crates"` co-development snippet;
- the README links `docs/releasing.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`.

The Rust examples still compile and run. Verified by `check-install-docs` and `check-doc-examples`.

New `docs/releasing.md` (no ```` ```rust ```` blocks, since `doc-examples` compiles every Rust block in `docs/*.md`) explains:

- how a release happens: Conventional Commits on `main` -> release-plz keeps a release PR (version bump in `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock`, new `CHANGELOG.md` section; edit its wording in the PR if needed) -> its CI runs -> check it, merge it -> the `release` job tags `vX.Y.Z` and creates the GitHub Release with that section as notes; the first release `v1.0.0` needed no PR;
- the **1.x policy**: `fix`/`perf` patch, `feat` minor, other types no release on their own; a breaking change is a `feat` (or `fix`) whose body states the break and whose `Migration:` footer holds the consumer note (rendered as *Migration:*); never `!` or `BREAKING CHANGE:` and `semver_check = false`, because release-plz would bump to 2.0.0 with no way to cap it; before merging a release PR its version must still be 1.x and it may change only `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` and `CHANGELOG.md` with green CI; a `2.0.0` proposal means a stray `!`/`BREAKING CHANGE:`, fixed by rewording that commit (force push allowed) instead of merging; the policy ends when a second consumer appears (then `!`, major bumps and the semver check return);
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN`: the shared fine-grained PAT (owner `rayx-ui`, Contents and Pull requests read/write, the library repositories in its access list), why the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` is not enough, its silent-expiry failure mode (red Release-plz runs, no release PR, no tag), that a repository missing from its access list fails at checkout/push, and rotation;
- the by-hand fallback: bump, changelog section, push, `git tag vX.Y.Z` on that commit, `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, and `gh release create vX.Y.Z` with the section as notes (release-plz normally creates the Release); release-plz continues from that tag;
- the "Release tags" ruleset: `v*` tags cannot be moved or deleted, so a bad release is fixed forward with a new patch;
- the push policy (fetch, rebase, `--force-with-lease`; pull requests only for releases; `ci.yml` runs on pull requests and pushes) and how consumers pin and bump a tag.

`docs/20-contributing.md` links it. Verified by review; `check-guidance` asserts the key terms and the absence of Rust blocks.
